1. Executive Summary
Team 3 built a distributed job-processing system and placed its client, server, and worker on a software-defined network. Clients submit jobs to a TCP server. The server assigns a unique ID, acknowledges the accepted job, stores it in a FIFO queue, and dispatches work to registered workers. Workers execute tasks and report completion. An OS-Ken controller programs OpenFlow switches in Mininet. D2 adds a primary and alternate network path and tests the system after a link failure.
In the submitted single-run benchmarks, adaptive routing completed 10/10 jobs after the primary link was disabled. Static primary-path routing completed 0/10 within its 15-second wait. These VM results demonstrate availability behavior; they are not a statistically controlled performance study.
2. Problem, objectives, and scope
The problem is to accept client work reliably, identify each submission, queue it while workers are busy, and report completion. The network should provide connectivity and, in D2, continue carrying traffic after a path failure.
•	Implement TCP client/server messaging with job IDs, acknowledgements, status, and results.
•	Queue jobs FIFO and assign them to available registered workers.
•	Connect Mininet hosts through Open vSwitch switches controlled by OS-Ken.
•	Add redundant network paths and compare adaptive failover with a static baseline.
•	Measure completion count, latency, and throughput.
