3. Architecture
The system has three layers. The application layer contains the TCP client, job server, scheduler, and worker. The data plane is Mininet with Open vSwitch. The control plane is OS-Ken, which communicates with switches using OpenFlow 1.3 and installs forwarding rules.
Node	Role	Address
h1	Job server	10.0.0.1, TCP port 5000
h2	Client / benchmark	10.0.0.2
h3	Worker	10.0.0.3
h4	Additional host	10.0.0.4
s1–s4	Open vSwitch	OpenFlow 1.3 controlled
OS-Ken	SDN controller	Learns locations and selects active paths
D2 uses a diamond network. h1 and h2 attach to s1; h3 and h4 attach to s4. The primary inter-switch path is s1–s2–s4; the alternate is s1–s3–s4. Adaptive mode tracks link state, clears stale route flows after a link event, and selects a path across active links. Static mode restricts routing to the primary path.
3.1 Job lifecycle and protocol
Step	Operation
1	Client sends a JSON job request over TCP.
2	Server assigns a sequential job ID, enqueues the job, and acknowledges receipt.
3	Scheduler selects an idle registered worker and dispatches the oldest job.
4	Worker simulates execution and reports its result to the server.
5	Server records status; benchmark client polls until completion or timeout.
Messages are newline-delimited JSON. TCP is a byte stream, so the newline marks the end of each application message. The shared protocol helper reads complete lines and applies a 1 MB message-size limit.
 
