Worker-flow test	Worker registers, receives three FIFO jobs and reports all results.
Concurrent clients	Eight clients receive acknowledgements with unique IDs.
Mininet baseline	nodes lists h1–h4 and s1–s4; pingall reports 0% dropped.
Worker registration	Server log reports worker1 from 10.0.0.3.
Normal benchmark	10 jobs complete and summary/CSV are produced.
Link failure	Disable s1–s2; adaptive completes on alternate path; static does not complete within the timeout.
 
6. Recorded benchmark results
Values below come from the supplied terminal outputs and CSV files. “Completed” counts jobs whose server status became completed within the benchmark wait window.
Scenario	Completed	Elapsed	Mean	P95	Rate
Static, normal	10/10	5.613 s	3.089 s	5.582 s	1.78 jobs/s
Static, primary link down	0/10	15.118 s	N/A	N/A	0 jobs/s
Adaptive, normal	10/10	5.818 s	3.184 s	5.778 s	1.72 jobs/s
Adaptive, after failover	10/10	5.777 s	3.157 s	5.745 s	1.73 jobs/s
The central D2 finding is that adaptive mode completed all ten jobs after the primary path was taken down, while the static baseline completed none before timeout. Normal-mode timings are close in this single run and do not prove a speed advantage.
6.1 CSV contents
Each CSV contains a scenario label, job ID, status, and completion time in seconds. Summary output aggregates completed jobs, elapsed duration, mean and P95 latency, and throughput. A job that remains queued or otherwise incomplete at timeout is not counted as completed.
7. Limitations and future work
•	Queue, job records and worker state are in memory, so restarting the server loses them.
•	Worker execution is simulated with a short delay rather than running arbitrary submitted programs.
•	Measurements are single VM runs with only ten jobs; repeated larger runs would produce stronger comparisons.
•	Static mode intentionally uses only the primary path.
•	Future improvements include durable storage, authentication, idempotent retry, worker health checks and resource-aware scheduling.
8. Reproduction overview
Use an Ubuntu VM with Mininet, Open vSwitch and OS-Ken. From a normal shell, start the adaptive controller in its own terminal with `cd sdn` and `python3 run_controller.py`. In another shell, clean and start Mininet using `sudo mn -c` and `sudo python3 sdn/topology.py`. At the `mininet>` prompt, run `nodes` and `pingall`; then start the server on h1 and worker on h3, and submit ten jobs from h2 with benchmark.py. Disable the primary link using `link s1 s2 down`, then repeat the adaptive benchmark. The project README contains exact commands.
Important: commands prefixed with h1/h2/h3 and `link ...` belong at the `mininet>` prompt. Controller commands run in a separate Ubuntu terminal. Exit Mininet with `exit` and clean stale networks with `sudo mn -c`.
9. Conclusion
The project demonstrates a TCP job-submission and worker-completion path integrated with an SDN-controlled Mininet network. D1 covers the queue and end-to-end job flow. D2 adds redundant network paths, adaptive OpenFlow failover, a static comparison, and measured job outcomes. The submitted evidence supports successful job completion after the tested primary-link failure in adaptive mode.
